The graph of $y = 3\tan(2x)$ and the line $y = -3$ are shown for $0 \le x \le \pi$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$},
  xmin=-0.2, xmax=3.5, ymin=-8.5, ymax=8.5,
  xtick={0.7854, 1.5708, 2.3562, 3.1416},
  xticklabels={$\frac{\pi}{4}$, $\frac{\pi}{2}$, $\frac{3\pi}{4}$, $\pi$},
  ytick={-6, -3, 3, 6},
  samples=100, clip=false,
  restrict y to domain=-8.5:8.5, unbounded coords=jump,
  axis line style={-{Stealth}}, width=11cm, height=6.5cm]
\addplot[smooth, thick, blue, domain=0:0.6060] {3*tan(deg(2*x))};
\addplot[smooth, thick, blue, domain=0.9648:2.1768] {3*tan(deg(2*x))};
\addplot[smooth, thick, blue, domain=2.5356:3.1416] {3*tan(deg(2*x))};
\addplot[thick, red, domain=0:3.1416] {-3};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Solve $3\tan(2x)+3 = 0$ for $0 \le x \le \pi$.
